.\" Manual page for tacctl, the management CLI for TACACS+ (tacquito) and RADIUS (FreeRADIUS).
.\" Render locally with: groff -man -Tascii man/tacctl.1 | less
.TH TACCTL 1 "2026-10-07" "tacctl 0.2.2" "tacctl Manual"
.SH NAME
tacctl \- manage users, groups and scopes for TACACS+ (tacquito) and RADIUS (FreeRADIUS)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tacctl
.I command
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ arguments... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tacctl
is a management CLI for network-device AAA. It keeps users,
groups, scopes (named CIDR + shared-secret bundles) and connection filters
in a store of its own,
.IR /etc/tacctl/store.yaml ,
and renders the configuration of every enabled
.I backend
from it: TACACS+ (RFC 8907) served by
.BR tacquito ,
installed by
.BR "tacctl install" ,
and, opt-in, RADIUS served by a tacctl-owned FreeRADIUS instance
.RB ( "tacctl backend enable radius" ).
It installs and upgrades the servers, inspects logs and backups, enrolls
Linux hosts (pam_tacplus or pam_radius_auth), and generates ready-to-paste
device configuration for Cisco IOS/IOS-XE and Juniper Junos, plus a
menu-by-menu setup procedure for WTI console servers, for either protocol.
See
.BR BACKENDS ,
.B "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION"
and
.BR UPGRADING .
.PP
Most commands require root.
.B tacctl
re-executes itself under
.BR sudo (8)
automatically when invoked by a non-root user.
.BR "tacctl hash" ,
.BR "tacctl version" ,
.B tacctl completion
and
.B tacctl shell
are deliberate exceptions: they run as the invoking user.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS System lifecycle
.TP
.BI "install [--branch " name "] [-y|--yes]"
Install tacctl and the TACACS+ backend. Builds the tacquito binary with the
Go toolchain (installed by
.I /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh
when it is missing), installs the
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl
binary (the verified release binary when the clone in
.I /opt/tacctl
is at a release tag, otherwise built from the clone),
installs the shell completion and this manual, creates the
.B tacquito
service user and the state directory
.IR /etc/tacctl ,
seeds the store (built-in groups and users, the scope
.B lab
with RFC 1918 prefixes and a generated shared secret, printed once),
renders
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
from it, installs the systemd units, and enables the service. An existing
store, or an existing
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
is kept instead (the latter goes through the upgrade's store gate). RADIUS
is not installed: see
.BR "backend enable" .
The optional
.BI --branch " name"
flag selects a specific tacctl repository branch for the install. The plan
is shown and confirmed
.RB ( [y/N] );
.B -y
(or
.BR --yes )
answers the confirmation, for scripts.
.IP
Four built-in users are seeded so fresh installs ship with usable
identities (without writing unknown credentials) and with a sink for
Junos internal accounting:
.B engineer
.RB ( superuser ),
.B operator
.RB ( operator ),
.B viewer
.RB ( readonly ),
and
.B root
.RB ( readonly ).
.IP
The first three carry a placeholder hash that never authenticates;
activate one with
.B "tacctl user passwd " <name>
(setting a real password implicitly enables the account).
.B root
is different — a permanent accounting-only sink whose hash stays
disabled for the lifetime of the install. It exists because Junos
internal daemons (mgd, jsd, op-script probes) run non-tty CLI commands
as root and generate accounting packets; without a
.B root
entry tacquito logs an error for every packet. Records of
.B root
with no terminal (port
.BR non\-tty ,
which Junos sends for them,
.B unknown
or empty) are answered with success and not recorded: those are the
device's own sessions, several a minute on Junos. A
.B root
login on the console or over ssh has a port and is recorded, its commands
included.
.B "tacctl user passwd root"
is rejected — root is a Junos built-in intended for local/console use
only and must never authenticate via TACACS+.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command install
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-branch\fR \fI<name>\fR
The tacctl repo branch to install or upgrade from (default: the current one).
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmations.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command install
.TP
.BI "upgrade [--branch " name "]"
Move tacctl state into
.I /etc/tacctl
(idempotent), pull and rebuild tacquito when upstream or the patch overlay
changed, pull the tacctl repository in
.I /opt/tacctl
(after switching to
.BI --branch " name"
when given) and, when the installed binary was not built from the commit
now checked out, replace it (the verified release binary at a release tag,
otherwise built from the clone) and re-execute it once, install
packages the new release needs, bring every enabled
backend's configuration in line with the release (a re-render from the
store; RADIUS restarts when its files or unit drop-in changed), refresh
system files (a device template you changed is kept; see
.I /etc/tacctl/templates/
under
.BR FILES ),
move an install that has no store yet into the store behind a
gate, and restart tacquito once if its binary, a unit or drop-in, or its
configuration changed (rolling the binary and unit files back if it does not
come up). The shell completion is regenerated by the binary and rewritten
only when its content changed (the output says
.B Updated
or
.BR Unchanged ).
A new README, logrotate file, completion or template restarts
nothing, and neither does an upgrade with nothing new. Listener, log-level and metrics
settings live in
.I tacctl.yaml
and survive. When the
.B engineer
group still carries the role preset of 0.2.2 (its Cisco rules, whose denies
did not hold, or its Junos sets), the upgrade ends with a red notice naming
.BR "group reset engineer" ;
the upgrade itself never changes an override. See
.BR UPGRADING .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command upgrade
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-branch\fR \fI<name>\fR
The tacctl repo branch to install or upgrade from (default: the current one).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command upgrade
.TP
.BI "rollback " version " [\-\-apply] [\-\-yes] [\-\-hosts]"
Prepare tacctl's state for the release before this one; run on the newer
release, before the older one is installed. A dry run by default: it lists
every step and every warning and changes nothing. The only version it takes is
.BR 0.2.2 ;
.B 0.2.1
and older are refused (0.2.2's own changes are not covered; restore a snapshot
with
.BR "backup restore" ),
and so is any other word.
.B \-\-apply
takes a snapshot first, then, in this order: removes from
.I tacctl.yaml
the key families 0.2.3 added
.RB ( linux.engineer_sudo ,
.BR snmp_scope.* ,
.BR breakglass_scope.* )
and any other key a 0.2.2 binary does not know (every
.BI tier. group
setting stays); removes
.B tiers.engineer
and
.B settings.space_completion
from
.I console.yaml
and writes it the way 0.2.2 reads it; removes the
.B location
of every device from
.IR devices.yaml ;
re-renders the enabled backends. It moves the per-scope SNMP credentials
.RI ( snmp/ scope .yaml )
aside to
.IR snmp.rolled\-back\-<timestamp>/
beside them, and removes the marker
.I /var/lib/tacctl/tier\-pinned
so that the upgrade that follows records the tier of a group made or raised
to priv-lvl 15 under 0.2.2. It leaves
.IR store.yaml ,
the host records and the engineer sshd drop-in
.IR 00\-tacctl\-engineer.conf ,
and lists them. The dry run names what needs a decision, and
.B \-\-apply
refuses without
.B \-\-yes
while any applies: the users who would become superusers under 0.2.2 (it
takes the tier from the priv-lvl band of the group, not from
.BI tier. group ),
the groups whose tier changes, and the settings that are dropped (per-scope
SNMP settings, break-glass users, the engineer sudo list, device locations;
they stay in the snapshot).
.B \-\-hosts
also syncs each enrolled Linux host (not this server's own entry) through
.B "host sync"
with
.B TAC_REVOKE_ENGINEER=1
in the client script's header (the protocol stays 6): the script writes no
.B %tac\-engineer
sudoers line and takes the engineer-tier accounts out of
.B tac\-superuser
and
.BR tac\-engineer ,
so engineers have no sudo there until the next 0.2.2 sync, which puts an
engineer at priv\-lvl 15 in
.B tac\-superuser
again (take engineer groups out of those scopes, or lower their priv\-lvl, first);
no other account is touched. The command
ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.2" ,
and the fallback that needs no tool: note the newest entry of
.B "backup list"
before upgrading (snapshots are taken before every change; the upgrade adds
one only when it records a tier), and
.B "backup restore"
that entry to come back. A second
.B \-\-apply
changes nothing. Superuser only. See
.BR UPGRADING .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command rollback
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-apply\fR
Take a snapshot, then convert the files 0.2.2 cannot read and re\-render.
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmations.
.TP
\fB\-\-hosts\fR
Also take the engineers' sudo off the enrolled Linux hosts.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command rollback
.TP
.BR "uninstall " [ -y | --yes ]
Remove tacctl and every backend that is enabled or installed: the tacquito
service, units, binaries, configuration and logs; tacctl's FreeRADIUS
configuration, unit drop-in and logs (the FreeRADIUS package stays, with its
own configuration as shipped); the state directory, sudoers rules,
completion, man page and management repository. Offers to preserve backups
and each backend's logs by archiving them to
.IR /root/ .
.B -y
(or
.BR --yes )
skips the confirmation and answers
.B no
to both offers: nothing is preserved.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command uninstall
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmations.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command uninstall
.TP
.B status
Show service health, authentication statistics, recent errors, and
password-age warnings. With more than one backend enabled each backend has a
labelled section
.RB ( "== Backend: <id> ..." )
holding its service, config, accounting log, drift and activity lines; the
users, backups, security posture and password-age lines that belong to no
backend stay in the general part. With one backend enabled the report is
laid out as a single report. A listener on an IPv6 network
.RB ( tcp6 ", " udp6 )
of any backend triggers the IPv6 ACL parity check.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command status
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command status
.TP
.BR "version " [ --long ]
.TQ
.BR --version ", " -v
Print the tacctl version, derived from
.BR git-describe (1)
when the binary was built, so it reflects the exact commit that was built.
.B --long
adds the commit, the build date, the Go version and whether the test knobs
are compiled in
.RB ( "test knobs: off" ).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command version
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-long\fR
Also the commit, build date, Go version and whether test knobs are on.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command version
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Print the completion script of that shell on stdout. The script asks
.B "tacctl __complete"
for the words, so completion always matches the installed binary. Live names
(users, groups, scopes, backends, listeners, backups) come from
.BR "sudo -n tacctl" ,
which needs root or the NOPASSWD rule of
.BR "config sudoers install" ;
without it only fixed words are completed. Install and upgrade write the
bash script to
.IR /etc/bash_completion.d/tacctl ;
the zsh and fish scripts are only printed: redirect them to
.I ~/.zfunc/_tacctl
(with that directory on
.B fpath
before
.BR compinit )
or to
.IR ~/.config/fish/completions/tacctl.fish .
.SS User management \fB(tacctl user)\fR
.TP
.B user list
List all users with name, Linux UID (\- when none is assigned), group, status,
password change date and scopes.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command user list
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command user list
.TP
.BI "user show " name
Show detailed user info: group, Linux UID, status, password change date and age,
last login (the most recent of every enabled backend: the TACACS+
accounting log, the RADIUS auth log), Cisco privilege level, Juniper
class, and a hash fingerprint. Read-only -- does not prompt for the
password (unlike
.BR "user verify" ).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command user show
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command user show
.TP
.BI "user add " "name group " [ "--hash " hash ] [ "--scopes " name[,name...] ]
Add a user. Prompts for the password with confirmation. If
.BI --hash " hash"
is given, the bcrypt hash is used directly and no password is prompted
(use this when users self-generate a hash; see
.BR "tacctl hash commands" ).
.BI --scopes " name"[,name...]
grants the user access to those scopes (see
.B SCOPE MANAGEMENT ).
Without
.BR --scopes ,
the user lands in the current default scope. On fresh installs the
default scope is
.B lab
— least-privilege posture, so an operator who forgets
.B --scopes
does not accidentally grant production access.
.PP
Reserved usernames
.RB ( root
and
.BR tacquito )
are rejected: both map to local OS accounts that tacquito resolves outside
the YAML. Create an operator-specific account instead (for example,
.BR "tacctl user add jsmith superuser" ).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command user add
.RS
