.SS Group management \fB(tacctl group)\fR
.TP
.B group list
List all groups with Cisco privilege level, Juniper class, and user count.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command group list
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command group list
.TP
.BI "group show " name
Every setting of the group and where it comes from (set on the group, or
the default of its priv-lvl): Cisco priv-lvl, Juniper class, tacctl tier,
WTI level, command rules, Junos rules and the number of users. The Junos
deny-commands and deny-configuration patterns are listed under the Junos
rules, for every tier; the hint
.B "group junos " \fIgroup\fR " list"
is printed only to a tier that may run it (the superuser).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command group show
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command group show
.TP
.B "group add " \fIname priv-lvl juniper-class\fR " [ --tier \fItier\fR ] [ --wti-level \fIlevel\fR ]"
Add a custom group.
.I priv-lvl
is an integer 0\(en15;
.I juniper-class
matches the Junos login class name.
.B --tier
and
.B --wti-level
set those two settings at creation (see
.BR "group edit" ).
A group at priv-lvl 15 other than the built-in
.B superuser
always has an explicit tier setting: without
.B --tier
the command records
.B superuser
(what the band gives; the output says so), so that a
.I tacctl.yaml
that later loses its settings cannot leave the group in doubt.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command group add
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-tier\fR \fI<tier>\fR
(add) Its tacctl tier instead of the priv\-lvl's (at 15: superuser if omitted).
.TP
\fB\-\-wti\-level\fR \fI<level>\fR
(add) Its WTI level instead of the priv\-lvl's.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command group add
.TP
.B "group edit " \fIname\fR " { priv-lvl \fIN\fR | juniper-class \fICLASS\fR | wti-level \fIlevel\fR | tier \fItier\fR }"
Change one setting of a group. Every group, built-in or custom, is edited
the same way; the built-in groups
.RB ( readonly ,
.BR operator ,
.BR superuser )
can be edited but not removed.
.B wti-level
is
.BR viewonly ,
.BR user ,
.B superuser
or
.BR administrator ,
the access level a WTI unit with Service Name
.B wti
gives the group's users;
.B tier
is
.BR readonly ,
.BR operator ,
.B engineer
or
.BR superuser ,
the group's tacctl tier on the server and on Linux hosts.
.B auto
removes either setting, so the priv-lvl decides again (below 7 readonly,
7\(en14 operator, 15 superuser); for the tier of a custom group at priv-lvl 15
it records
.B superuser
instead. A group other than the built-in
.B superuser
always has a tier setting at priv-lvl 15 (the built-in
.B readonly
and
.B operator
included):
.B "edit priv-lvl"
records
.B superuser
when it raises a group into the band without one, and removes a
.B superuser
setting when it lowers the group out of it, whether the setting only recorded
the band or was chosen with
.B --tier superuser
(set the tier again when a superuser group below 15 is wanted; an
.BR engineer ,
.B operator
or
.B readonly
setting stays). A group at priv-lvl 15 with no tier setting
at all (the settings were lost: an empty or restored
.IR tacctl.yaml )
is ambiguous: its members are held at the operator tier, the denial names
the group,
.BR "host enroll" " and " "host sync"
give them the operator tier on the hosts and print one red line per group
naming the repair (they are not refused: a sync that takes rights away still
runs), and
.B "group show"
says
.BR "NOT SET" ;
root, or a superuser who is not a member, sets its tier with
.BR "group edit" .
A change of
.B tier
or
.B priv-lvl
(and
.BR "user move" ", " "user disable" ", " "user remove" ", a " "user scope"
change that takes this server's scope away,
.BR "group preset roles --force" ", and " "group reset" )
that lowers the tier of a user who has an account on the tacctl server
syncs those accounts at once, as
.B "host sync"
of the host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
does, so the user does not keep the old tier's groups
.RB ( tac\-superuser
among them); when the sync fails or no server is enrolled, the command says
which users keep their old groups until
.BR "host sync" .
A raise syncs nothing.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command group edit
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command group edit
.TP
.BI "group remove " name
Remove a custom group. Built-in groups are protected.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command group remove
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command group remove
.TP
.B "group commands list " \fIgroup\fR
Show the per-command authorization rules for a group, in the order
tacquito tries them (the
.B #
column), including the
default action (the action of the trailing
.RB ` * `
catchall rule).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command group commands list
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command group commands list
.TP
.B "group commands default " \fIgroup\fR " { permit | deny }"
Set the default action for unmatched commands. Stored as the action
of a trailing
.RB ` * `
rule that tacquito's
.B CommandBasedAuthorizer
matches as a wildcard.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command group commands default
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command group commands default
.TP
.B "group commands add " \fIgroup\fR " " \fIname\fR " [ --match \fIregex\fR ]... [ --action permit | deny ] [ --before \fIname\fR | --first ]"
Add a rule before the
.RB ` * `
catchall, or with
.B --before
before the first rule of that name, or with
.B --first
at position 1. Default action is
.BR permit .
The
.I name
is matched against the TACACS+
.B cmd=
field literally. Each
.B --match
adds a regex that tacquito tests against the command's
.I arguments
only: the space-joined
.B cmd-arg
values after the command word, without
.B <cr>
(tacctl renders every regex as
.RB ` ^(?:…)$ `,
so it must match the whole arguments string and a top-level alternation
stays together:
.B ^crypto
matches
.B show crypto
only; write
.B "^crypto( .*)?$"
to cover the arguments after it too).
For
.B show running-config
the string tested is
.BR running-config ,
never the full line, so a regex that repeats the command word
.RB ( "^show .*$" )
can never match and is rejected. A regex may not contain a comma (the
rule's stored line form separates matches with commas); write
.B \ex2c
for one, nor be empty (tacquito skips an empty regex), nor be invalid
(checked with Go's RE2, as tacquito does). Omit
.B --match
to cover any arguments. A warning follows the added rule when a
regex is a prefix form without
.B "( .*)?"
(it matches the exact arguments only) or when the rule can never be
reached because an earlier rule of the same name has no
.B --match
or follows the
.RB ` * `
catchall;
.B "config validate"
warns about the same in the stored rules.
Rules are tried in order: a rule without
.B --match
decides, one whose regexes all miss falls through to the next, and tacquito
returns FAIL if no rule decides (and the
.RB ` * `
