.TP
.B "host sync " "{ " name " | \-\-all } [ \-\-allow\-uid\-mismatch ] [ \-\-remove\-home ]"
Push the scope's current user list to the host: new users get accounts,
tier changes move group membership, disabled users' accounts are expired
(files kept; enabling the user restores the account), and the accounts of
users no longer in the scope (or no longer tacctl users) are deleted with
.BR userdel (8),
with an own group an earlier release made when it is empty. Their UIDs stay
reserved on the server. Run after
.BR "user add" ", " "user remove" ", " "user move" ", " "user disable" " or " "user scope"
changes.
.IP
tacctl's groups have fixed GIDs, the first numbers of the range:
.B tac\-users
(+0, every host: every managed account's primary group and the PAM gate),
.B tac\-console
(+1, the tacctl server only: sshd's console drop-in),
.B tac\-superuser
(+2, every host: sudo),
.B tac\-operator
(+3) and
.B tac\-readonly
(+4) (the tacctl server only: its tiers sudoers). Accounts have no group
of their own and get a 0700 home. A sync moves what an earlier release made:
primary groups to
.BR tac\-users ;
own groups removed when empty; groups moved to their fixed GIDs
.RB ( "groupmod \-g" );
on a host other than the server, the tier and console groups removed when
only tacctl's accounts are in them. A fixed GID another group holds is left
to it, with a warning.
.IP
tacctl manages an account only when it created it on that host and its UID
there is in the server's range (80000\(en89999 by default; see
.BR "config linux uid\-range" ). Any other account is never created, expired,
deleted or otherwise changed, with one exception: it is taken out of
tacctl's own groups
.RB ( tac\-users ", " tac\-readonly ", " tac\-operator ", " tac\-superuser ", " tac\-console )
and nothing else on it changes, so it is a plain local account. A local
account named like a user of tacctl that tacctl did not create is otherwise
left as it is, and that user gets no account on that host (a warning says
so); the rest of the sync goes on. The summary counts the accounts tacctl
manages on the host and names the refused users, as in
.RB \(lq "c1: synced (4 users; 1 refused: carl)." \(rq.
.IP
An account tacctl created with a UID of a range the server used before
(20000\(en29999, where releases up to 0.2.0 gave them out, or the range before a
.BR "config linux uid\-range" )
is renumbered first, to the same offset in the current range: its UID
.RB ( "usermod \-u" ,
which re-owns the home directory tree)
.RI "(\(lq'" user "': renumbered " old " \-> " new " (home re\-owned)\(rq;"
the summary counts them, as in
.RB \(lq "c1: synced (3 users; 1 renumbered)." \(rq).
Files under
.IR /home ", " /tmp ", " /var/tmp ", " /var/spool/cron " and " /var/mail
that still carry the old number are listed (at most 20), never changed. An
account whose user is logged in, or whose new UID is taken on the
host, is left as it is and named as refused until a later sync.
.IP
A removed user's home directory is deleted only when asked for. From a
terminal, enroll and sync read the host's
.B "getent passwd"
over the same ssh connection (read-only, before anything runs there) and ask
.RI "\(lqDelete /home/" user " of removed user '" user "'? [y/N]\(rq"
for each removed user;
.B \-\-remove\-home
deletes them all without asking; with no terminal and no flag every home is
kept. A kept home is moved to
.IR /home/.tacctl\-removed/ user \- YYYYmmdd \- HHMMSS
(the directory is root's, mode 0700; the moved tree is made root:root with
.BR "chown \-hR" ,
never following a link), so a local account that later gets the same UID
does not inherit it; the host prints
.RI "\(lqhome kept: /home/.tacctl\-removed/" user \- time \(rq.
A home that is not a directory directly under
.IR /home ,
is a symbolic link, is not owned by the account, is also another
account's home or is on another file system is neither deleted nor moved
.RI "(\(lqhome kept in place: " home " (" reason ")\(rq)."
.IP
Enroll and sync also read the host's
.I /etc/login.defs
(read-only) and warn when its
.BR UID_MIN \(en UID_MAX
range overlaps the server's range, where local
.BR useradd (8)
could give out a UID tacctl has assigned (the default
.B "UID_MAX 60000"
does not overlap 80000\(en89999); keep it clear of the range there. tacctl
never edits the file. Before anything runs there, enroll and sync read the
host's
.I /proc/self/uid_map
and
.IR gid_map ,
and refuse a host whose user namespace cannot hold the range
.RI "(\(lq'" host "' cannot hold UIDs " min \- max ": its user namespace maps only " ranges " (an unprivileged container). \&...\(rq)."
.IP
Enroll and sync record the address the ssh connection reached (the host's
side of
.BR SSH_CONNECTION ;
.B \-\-local
records 127.0.0.1) as the host's address in the device registry:
.B "device add"
refuses it, sightings from it are the host's, and
.B "device discover"
does not list it. A sync that finds another address records it, logs
.BR "host address\-changed" ,
and raises the
.B address\-changed
notice
.RB ( "device notice " \fIname\fP " ack address\-changed" ).
.IP
A host with no pinned host key gets the keys it offers pinned, as at
enrollment. A pin is never changed by a sync: a key that differs from the
pinned one is reported, with the pinned and offered fingerprints, and new key
types are reported; both are fixed with
.BI "device hostkey " name " accept" \fR.
