without locking tacctl out of it.
.B rotate
is the only subcommand. One rotation of a host runs at a time on the tacctl
server (a lock under
.IR /etc/tacctl/locks ,
held for the whole command): a second one fails at once with
"another rotation of <name> is running" and changes nothing.
Nothing is changed until everything that can be
checked here has been: the host is enrolled and not
the server's own
.B \-\-local
entry; the new account is not
.BR root ,
the login in use, or a user of tacctl; exactly one of
.B \-\-key
and
.B \-\-password
is given (the latter needs a terminal, and a host with pinned ssh host keys: with
none, the password would be typed to a peer nothing has authenticated);
and
.B \-\-remove\-old
has a registry target with an explicit
.IR user @
to name the old account. Then, in this order: (1) the new account is created
over the login in use by a small script of its own (not the
.B config linux
script), below the first number of tacctl's UID range and out of the ranges
the server used before (its group too), with
.B "useradd \-m \-U \-s /bin/bash \-c 'tacctl provisioning account'"
(that comment, the account's GECOS field, is for display: the account can
change it itself, or it comes from a directory service, so nothing is decided
by it). Root's own proof that
tacctl made the account is a record the script writes at once under
.I /var/lib/tacctl\-provisioner
(a directory of root's alone, 0700; one file per account with its name and UID).
A re\-run adopts an existing account only when it is in
.I /etc/passwd
itself (not one an sssd or LDAP directory supplies) and that record agrees; any
other account of the name is refused. The sudoers line
.RI ( /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl\-provisioner ,
checked with
.BR visudo (8);
never
.IR tacctl\-host ,
which each sync rewrites) is the sudoers line last: it is written only after the key (or the password) is in
place, under a lock on the host, so two scripts at once lose no line; a failure
takes back what the run did, the line included; (2) the new login is proved with a fresh ssh in a
NEW connection (no shared connection) that runs
.B "sudo id \-u"
and must get
.BR 0 .
The proof's ssh trusts the host's pinned ssh keys and no others:
tacctl writes them to a temporary known_hosts file for the run and gives ssh
.BR "UserKnownHostsFile=<that file>" ", " GlobalKnownHostsFile=/dev/null ", "
.BR StrictHostKeyChecking=yes " and " HostKeyAlias=<name> ,
so another machine is refused by ssh itself (and the keys the login reads are
compared with the pins as well, as
.B "host target"
does; a difference is logged
.RB ( "auth.warning host provisioner hostkey\-mismatch name=" )).
The proof's ssh options are stricter than a later login's (one key only,
no GSSAPI or host\-based logins). A host with no pinned keys has nothing to
trust: the proof then goes by the invoking user's own known_hosts, lists the
keys the host answered with and asks you to confirm them
.RB ( \-\-yes
confirms), and
.B \-\-password
is refused; (3) only then the registry line is read again and rewritten as
.B "host target"
does (after a snapshot; scope, server and method kept, a
.B "host move"
that ran meanwhile kept; a change of how the host is reached stops the
rotation), the old login is
recorded in the host's record, and the rotation is logged
.RB ( "host provisioner rotate name= old= new= auth=key|password by=" );
(4) with
.BR \-\-remove\-old ,
the old account is removed last, over the new one: its sudoers line first,
then its processes end,
.BR userdel (8)
runs, and its home directory is moved out of
reach and made root\-owned, or deleted with
.BR \-\-remove\-home .
A terminal is asked about the home when neither is given. The removal is
refused when the old and new accounts are the same, the old one is
.B root
or a user of tacctl, an account with a number in tacctl's UID range, or the
registry does not point at the new account yet.
.IP
With
.BI \-\-key " file"
the account has a locked password,
.B NOPASSWD: ALL
and the public key in its
.I ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
(modes 0700 and 0600,
.BR restorecon (8)
where SELinux is on). The key is written by the account itself
.RB ( runuser (1)),
never by root inside a directory the account owns, and
.I ~/.ssh
is refused when it is a link, belongs to somebody else or is writable by its
group or others. An adopted account's
.I ~/.ssh
is emptied first (what an earlier user left: rc, authorized_keys2) and is
adopted only when tacctl made that directory; the rest of the home
(dotfiles, cron, user units) is not scanned, so an account that has been in
use is better removed and made again. The proof runs
.B "sudo \-n id \-u"
with password and keyboard\-interactive logins off. A missing file is made by
.B "ssh\-keygen \-t ed25519"
(which asks for the passphrase; empty is the operator's choice). Every access
to the key file (existence, owner, the public key, the fingerprint) runs as the
invoking user, never as root, and its path is recorded as the registry identity
as
.B "host target \-\-identity"
does. With
.B \-\-password
the account gets
.B ALL
with a password the operator types into the host's own
.BR passwd (1)
over the ssh terminal; the proof runs
.B "sudo id \-u"
with public key logins off and the operator typing the password. The
password never passes through tacctl: not its arguments, its environment, a
file, a log or the audit line. A host whose sshd has
.B "PasswordAuthentication no"
always fails a
.B \-\-password
proof.
.IP
