Every walkthrough of
.BR "config cisco" ", " "config juniper " "and " "config wti"
has an SNMP step, built from the settings of the scope with the default
beneath them (see
.BR "scope snmp" ):
read-only access for the tacctl server's own address first, the scope's
allowed client ranges in the order given, and everything else
.RB ( 0.0.0.0/0 )
refused last, always. On Cisco that is the standard access list
.BR TACCTL\-SNMP
(a /32 as
.BR "permit host" ,
the others with wildcard masks, ending in
.BR "deny any" )
bound to
.B "snmp-server community ... RO"
or to a v3 group and user (authPriv), with
.B snmp-server location
and
.BR contact ;
on Junos a
.B client-list
of the same name with
.BR "0.0.0.0/0 restrict" ,
the read-only community bound to it or the v3 usm user with its vacm group and
view, and
.B "set snmp location|contact|description"
(the client list restricts communities only; a v3 user is not restricted by it,
which is not verified on a device; the management filter of Step 4, when it is
rendered, has permit-snmp and deny-snmp terms for udp port 161 from the same
clients, which restrict v3 once the filter is applied to lo0); on WTI the unit's SNMP menu in the
walkthrough's style, whose menu names and client restriction (the udp port 161
lines of the unit's IP Tables list, Step 5) are from WTI's documents and not
verified on a unit. The
community or the v3 passphrases are printed for superusers and for engineers
(of their own scopes). A value that is not set (the scope's contact, a
device's location) is a commented placeholder, never text a paste would
apply, and the output ends with
.BI "Unfilled SNMP values: contact (tacctl scope snmp " scope " contact '<text>'), location (tacctl device location " name " '<text>')" .
An SNMP version unset in the scope and in the default makes the step a comment
saying SNMP is not configured.
.B \-\-name
takes the SNMP location, description and sysName of a registered device of the
scope
.RB ( "device location" ,
.BR "device description" );
.B "--snmp-location"
gives the location for this paste only (not stored). The Cisco walkthrough ends
with a commented
.B netconf-yang
step and its prerequisite (exec authorization), for superusers; an engineer's
says to ask one; IOS 12.x has none. The Junos walkthrough has a commented
.B "set system services netconf ssh"
step with the optional connection and rate limits and the check
.RB ( "ssh -p 830 -s" " user@device " netconf ),
and its management filter permits tcp port 830 next to ssh; the WTI unit has
no NETCONF. Nothing is enabled by tacctl itself.
.B "--server"
.IR address|name
is the address the devices are told to authenticate against (TACACS+ or RADIUS
server lines), for devices that reach this server through a translating
firewall; a host name must resolve to an IPv4 address.
.B "--source"
