.TP
.BI "backend status " [ id ]
Service and listener state of every backend (or one): state, uptime, PID,
and for each listener its network and address, the state of its unit and
whether something is listening there. Read-only.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command backend status
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command backend status
.TP
.BI "backend enable " "id " [ \-y ]
Enable a backend. If it is not installed, confirms (\fB\-y\fR skips) and runs its
install steps (packages, service account, unit). Then, as one step: every
backend that will be enabled has its gate checked (a hand-edited rendered file
refuses the command, exit 3), a snapshot is taken, the id is added to
.BR backends.enabled ,
and the config of every enabled backend is rendered, all or none. Then the
service is enabled at boot and started, and must report
.BR active .
If any step fails, the files tacctl owns
.RB ( tacctl.yaml ,
the store, every rendered file) are left or put back exactly as they were, the
service is stopped and disabled, and the exit status is 1; software that the
install step put on the machine stays.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command backend enable
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command backend enable
.TP
.BI "backend disable " "id " [ \-y ]
Take a backend out of
.BR backends.enabled ,
stop its service and disable it at boot. Asks for confirmation (\fB\-y\fR
skips) because clients of that protocol stop authenticating. Its package and
the files it rendered stay where they are (and are no longer rendered or
reported as drift);
.B "backend enable"
brings it back. Refused for the last enabled backend (nothing would serve the
store: enable another first, or use
.BR uninstall ),
and for
.B tacacs
on an install that has no store yet (legacy read-only mode, where
.I tacquito.yaml
is still the source of truth).
.IP
Commands need the store
.RI ( /etc/tacctl/store.yaml ),
or, on an install from before the store, its
.IR tacquito.yaml ;
with neither they stop with \(lqConfig not found\(rq.
.I tacquito.yaml
is the
.B tacacs
backend's file, rendered from the store: a RADIUS-only install has none
and runs every command without it. With
.B tacacs
enabled and
.I tacquito.yaml
missing, commands run and warn;
.B "tacctl config render"
(or any change) writes it again.
.PP
.B backend list
and
.B backend status
are available to the read-only and operator tiers;
.B enable
and
.B disable
are superuser only.
