.SS Interactive shell \fB(tacctl shell)\fR
.TP
.BI "shell " "[ \-\-no\-history ] [ \-\-idle " min " ] [ \-\-space\-completion { on | off } ] [ \-c " line " ]"
A prompt,
.BR "tacctl> " ,
where each line is a tacctl command without the
.B tacctl
in front:
.BR "user list" ,
.BR "scope show lab" .
The shell runs as the invoking user and holds no privilege: each line runs as
.B sudo
.RB [ \-n ]
.I tacctl words
with the terminal attached, so sudo's policy, its log line and tacctl's tier
gate apply to every line exactly as from a login shell, and prompts, password
input and Ctrl-C work as they do there.
.B \-n
is used for members of
.B tac-users
in the readonly or operator tier, or in no tier group (they have no local
password and their rules are NOPASSWD); a line that sudo refuses them and
that their tier's sudoers rules do not cover is reported as not permitted for
the tier. A member of
.B tac-superuser
runs plain
.BR sudo ,
which asks for the network password on the terminal when a line needs it
(once; sudo's cache then applies), so superusers' write verbs work from the
shell.
.B hash
and
.B completion
lines run without sudo.
For
.B host
lines the agent socket
.RB ( SSH_AUTH_SOCK )
is passed as it is when tacctl re-executes itself under sudo.
Messages and exit statuses are those of the command; after a command that
ends with a non-zero status the shell prints
.BR "[exit " \fIN\fB ] .
.IP
A line is split into words at blanks (space, tab); single quotes, double
quotes and backslash quote as in
.BR sh (1),
and nothing else is special: there are no variables, globbing, pipes,
redirections, command separators, command substitution or history expansion
(\c
.BR ; ,
.BR | ,
.BR $( ,
backticks,
.BR > ,
.B !
are ordinary characters of a word). A line is at most 4096 bytes.
The shell's own words are
.B help
(the top-level usage),
.BI "help " command
(the usage of that command's family),
.B history
(the lines entered, as stored),
.B exit
and
.BR quit .
.IP
Editing keys: Left/Right, Home/End (Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E), Esc-b and Esc-f (or
Alt-Left, Alt-Right) by word, Backspace, Delete, Ctrl-K, Ctrl-U, Ctrl-W,
Ctrl-T, Ctrl-L; Up/Down (Ctrl-P, Ctrl-N) step through the history and
Ctrl-R searches it backwards as you type (Ctrl-R again: further back; Enter
runs the match; Ctrl-G gives the line back). Tab completes commands,
sub-commands, flags and names (users, groups, scopes, hosts, devices, ...,
asked of
.B sudo \-n tacctl _completion-names
and kept for 5 seconds); a second Tab lists the matching words alone, in
columns that fit the terminal. ? inserts nothing and shows help for the
cursor's position: where words can come, \(lqPossible completions:\(rq with
each one's argument column and description (a device or host with its
vendor, address and scope); after a command that takes arguments, its usage
lines, its options and what comes next (\(lqNext: <username> <group>\(rq,
\(lqNext: <Enter> to run\(rq). Inside quotes, or after a backslash, ? is
typed as a character. A list of more than 40 entries asks first
(\(lqShow all 45 devices? [y/N]\(rq): y shows it, any other key (or
Ctrl-C) prints how to narrow it and gives the line back. Help from ? taller
than the terminal is shown a screenful at a time (\(lq\-\- more (Space:
page, Enter: line, q: quit) \-\-\(rq; q or Ctrl-C stops). A paste is inserted as one line (its newlines become
blanks, at most 4096 bytes) and runs only on Enter. Ctrl-C at the prompt discards the
line; Ctrl-C while a command runs ends the command, and the shell carries on.
Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-\e do nothing: there is no job control, and the commands
inherit that. Ctrl-D on an empty line, like
.BR exit ,
leaves the shell.
.IP
Tab, ?, a typed space and the shell's top-level
.B help
list only the commands and sub-commands that the caller's tier can run
(the rows of the tier table, which the sudoers drop-in is written from, so
the two cannot differ): a readonly user does not see
.B store
or
.BR install ,
an operator does not see
.BR host ,
and a superuser and a caller outside
.B tac-users
see everything. The list ends with \(lqShown: the commands the
.I tier
tier can run\(rq.
The usage stays complete:
.BI "help " command
describes every verb of the family and ends with the tier each one needs (with
a note for the verbs whose sub-verbs the code splits:
.BR "scope secret" ,
.BR "scope staging" ,
.BR "host default\-method" ,
.BR "device import" ),
and ? after a verb typed by hand names the tier it needs
(\(lqNeeds the \fItier\fR tier.\(rq; after a command family the tier cannot run at
all, \(lqNeeds the \fItier\fR tier. help \fIfamily\fR describes it.\(rq). The
descriptions of the rows that are listed are the usage's text and can still name
a verb of the family that is not listed. This is display only:
the tier gate and sudo remain the check, and a command typed by hand is
refused as before. The shell learns the tier of a
.B tac-users
member from the root side
.RB ( "sudo \-n tacctl _console-policy" ,
as the console does) when it starts, in the background and within 3 seconds, so
typing never waits for it; until the answer is in, or when it cannot be
learned, only the read-only commands are listed, and the next Tab, ? or space
asks again once 30 seconds have passed. A disabled tacctl user (the root side
answers that the account has no tier) is listed no command, as the gate refuses
them all;
.BR help ,
.B exit
and
.B quit
remain.
This manual and the usage printed by
.B tacctl
itself are not filtered: they show everything.
.IP
The history is kept in
.I ~/.local/state/tacctl/history
(mode 0600, the newest 1000 lines).
A line is stored redacted: everything after
.BR \-\-secret ,
.B \-\-hash
or
.BR \-\-password ,
after
.B set
in a
.B secret ... set
line, after
.B import
when a value follows it, and after the word of a
.B passwd
line is replaced by
.BR \[u2026](redacted) ;
.B "scope secret lab set x"
is stored as
.BR "scope secret lab set \[u2026](redacted)" .
A recalled line that carries the mark is refused; type the value again.
Lines ended with Ctrl-C are not stored.
.B \-\-no\-history
keeps the history of the session in memory only.
.IP
.BR "\-\-space\-completion on" | off
(default on) makes a typed space work as on a Junos device. A space is
refused (nothing is inserted) at the start of the line, right after a space and
right before one. At the end of a word that is a command, a sub-command, a
fixed choice or a flag name, it completes the word when exactly one such word
starts with what is typed and adds the blank
.RB ( de
and a space give
.BR "device " );
when several do, they are listed once as ? lists them, without the question
for a long list and without paging, and nothing is inserted (more than
40 words, or the console's
.BR list_max ,
print nothing). A word that is complete already
.RB ( user
while
.B user\-x
exists), a name (user, group, scope, host, device, file), free text, a typo and
a space in the middle of a word get the space as typed. Inside quotes, after a
backslash, in a Ctrl-R search, and with Tab and ?, nothing changes. A paste is
not affected: it is inserted as it is, with the setting on or off. The login
console reads the
setting from
.BR "console space\-completion" .
.B \-c
and batch input are not affected.
.B off
turns off the refusal of repeated spaces and the completion: every typed space
is a space.
.IP
.BI "\-\-idle " min
ends the shell after
.I min
minutes at the prompt without input (0, the default: never; at most 1440);
the time a command runs does not count.
.IP
.BI "\-c " line
runs one line and exits with its status; the line is stored in the history.
With standard input not a terminal (\c
.BR "tacctl shell < file" ),
the shell runs the lines of the input in order, with no prompt and no
history, and stops at the first line that ends with a non-zero status,
exiting with that status. The commands of such a batch get no standard input
(it is the script), so a command that asks a question cannot be answered
from it: use its
.B \-y
flag where it has one.
Over ssh, ask for a terminal to get the interactive shell:
.BR "ssh \-t " "host tacctl shell" ;
without
.B \-t
ssh allocates none, and the shell reads commands from standard input and
says so on standard error.
Bare
.B tacctl
on a terminal still prints the usage and exits 1.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command shell
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-history\fR
Keep no history file for this session.
.TP
\fB\-\-idle\fR \fI<min>\fR
End the session after this many idle minutes at the prompt.
.TP
\fB\-\-space\-completion\fR \fIon|off\fR
A typed space completes a fixed word and never doubles (default on).
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fI<line>\fR
Run one line and exit.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command shell
.SH SCOPE MANAGEMENT
A
.I scope
is a named bundle of (client CIDR prefixes, shared secret), kept in the
store and shared by every backend: the same prefixes and secret serve
TACACS+ and RADIUS, unless the scope's
.B protocols
filter limits it. Each user's
.B scopes
list references one or more of these by name. A client is matched to the
scope with the most specific prefix that contains its address (tacquito
walks its rendered
.B secrets:
list, sorted by specificity, first match wins; FreeRADIUS takes the longest
prefix); authentication succeeds only when that scope is in the user's
list.
.PP
Fresh installs seed a single scope named
.B lab
(least-privilege default). Operators add additional scopes (for
example
.BR prod ,
.BR edge ,
.BR dmz )
as needed, and place users into them explicitly.
.SS Top-level \fB(tacctl scope)\fR
.TP
.B scope list
One row per scope (deduplicated), with each scope's prefixes joined
as a comma list. Handy for a high-level inventory — names, prefix
counts, user counts, and default marker. For the first-match routing
view (one row per scope-prefix pair, numbered by slice position,
mirroring how tacquito routes a connecting client) use
.BR "scope routing" .
For full per-scope detail (secret posture, per-scope knobs, user list) use
.BR "scope show " \fIname\fR .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope list
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope list
.TP
.B scope routing
Print every (scope, prefix) pair in first-match order, as rendered into
tacquito's
.B secrets[]
list (one row per scope-prefix pair,
numbered by slice position). Multi-prefix scopes repeat their name on
each prefix row so the display literally mirrors routing priority —
reading top to bottom shows the order tacquito tries providers for a
connecting client.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope routing
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope routing
.TP
.BI "scope show " name
Full detail for one scope: prefixes, users that reference it, the
shared secret's length and posture (never its value:
.BI "scope secret " name " show"
prints that), whether the scope is
the default, its protocols filter and auth-method, its vendor attributes
and tagged addresses (RADIUS).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope show
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope show
.TP
.B "scope add " \fIname\fR " --prefixes " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...] " [ --secret \fIvalue\fR | --secret generate ] [ --protocols \fIcsv\fR ] [ --vendor-attrs \fIcsv\fR ] [ --default ]
Create a new scope. Prefixes are required; each must not already
belong to another scope (overlap with a broader scope's supernet is
fine — the new scope's narrower prefix will win first-match).
.B --secret generate
produces a strong key automatically.
.B --default
makes the new scope the default for
.BR "user add" .
.B --protocols
and
.B --vendor-attrs
set what
.B "scope protocols set"
and
.B "scope vendor-attrs enable"
would.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope add
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-prefixes\fR \fI<cidrs>\fR
(add) The scope's CIDRs, comma\-separated.
.TP
\fB\-\-secret\fR \fI<value>\fR
(add) The shared secret, or a generated one.
.TP
\fB\-\-protocols\fR \fI<csv>\fR
(add) Limit it to tacacs, radius (default: all).
.TP
\fB\-\-vendor\-attrs\fR \fI<csv>\fR
(add) RADIUS vendor attributes to send (cisco, juniper, wti).
.TP
\fB\-\-default\fR
(add) Make it the default scope.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope add
.TP
.BI "scope remove " "name " [ --force ]
Remove a scope from the store (and so from every backend's rendered
config). Refuses by default if any user references the
scope; with
.BR --force ,
strips the name from all affected users AND removes the scope,
with a loud warning. Its vendor attributes and tagged addresses go with it. Every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the scope's name
.RB ( aaa.order ,
.BR exec_timeout ,
.BR tacacs_group ,
.BR radius_group ,
.BR scope_auth_method ,
.B scope_mgmt_acl.names.cisco|juniper
and
.BR scope_mgmt_acl.permits ),
its SNMP settings
.RB ( snmp_scope.\fIscope\fR.* )
and its SNMP credentials file are removed with it, so a new scope of that
name starts from the defaults.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope remove
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
(remove) Also take it out of the users that have it.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope remove
.TP
.BI "scope rename " "old new"
Rename a scope. Rewrites the scope in the store (protocols, vendor
attributes and tagged addresses included), every user's
.B scopes
reference, and
.B scope.default
in
.I tacctl.yaml
(if it pointed at
.IR old ),
and moves every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the old name to the new one.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope rename
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope rename
.TP
.BI "scope default " [ name ]
Show (no arg) or set (with arg) the default scope. Stored under
.B scope.default
in
.IR /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml .
Used by
.BR "user add"
when
.B --scopes
is omitted, and by
.BR "config cisco" " / " "config juniper" " / " "config wti"
when
.B --scope
is omitted.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope default
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope default
.TP
.BI "scope lookup " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Resolve an IPv4 / IPv6 address or CIDR to the scope that owns it,
following the same slice-order walk tacquito's provider selector uses.
Prints the matching prefix and, if multiple scopes' prefixes cover the
query (e.g. a supernet/subnet overlap), lists the shadowed scopes
below the winner for debugging. An address the scope has tagged with a
vendor
.RB ( "scope devices" )
is shown with its tag.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope lookup
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope lookup
.SS Per-scope prefixes \fB(tacctl scope prefixes)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "list"
Show the scope's current prefixes.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope prefixes
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
(remove) Remove every prefix of the scope, which removes the scope.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
(remove \-\-all) Also take the scope out of the users that have it.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope prefixes
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " add " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Add one or more CIDRs. Each CIDR is canonicalized
.RB ( 10.1.5.5/24 " -> " 10.1.5.0/24 ),
checked against the one-CIDR-per-scope invariant (rejected with the
owning-scope name if already claimed elsewhere), and appended as its
own
.B secrets[]
entry in the rendered
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
where every entry is sorted globally by prefix
specificity so tacquito's slice-ordered first-match walk routes each
client to the narrowest scope that contains it (and its own RADIUS client).
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " remove " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Remove one or more CIDRs. The last prefix of a scope cannot be removed
(use
.B "remove \-\-all"
or
.BR "scope remove" ),
and a prefix whose removal would leave a tagged address
.RB ( "scope devices" )
outside the scope is refused.
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "remove \-\-all " [ \-\-force ]
Empty the entire prefix list for the scope (with confirmation): the scope
and its per-scope settings are removed, so it vanishes from every rendered
config. The command refuses to proceed when users reference the scope
unless
.B \-\-force
is given, which strips the scope from those users.
.B \-\-all
takes no CIDRs, and
.B \-\-force
is only valid with it. A scope enrolled Linux hosts use is not removed
(move them first:
.BR "host move" ).
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " move " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...] " " \fIother\fR
Move the CIDRs from
.I scope
to
.I other
in one change, so no address they hold is left unanswered. The same checks
as remove and add apply. Enrolled hosts whose addresses another scope then
answers are named, with the
.B "host move"
that follows them; none is moved.
.SS Staging addresses \fB(tacctl scope staging)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope staging " "[ list | remove " address " ]"
A host or network device provisioned off-site for a scope, at a bench
address the scope does not cover
.RB ( "host enroll ... \-\-scope " "scope " "\-\-staging" ,
.BR "config cisco" | juniper | wti " \-\-scope " "scope " "\-\-staging " "ip " [ "\-\-name " device ]),
has that address added to the scope as a /32: the scope's own secret and
users answer it there, so nothing changes on the device when it is
installed in the scope's prefixes. The /32 is removed once the host (its
enroll or sync) or the registered device
.RB ( "device set" )
is seen at another address the scope covers.
.B list
(the default) shows them with where each is seen now;
.B remove
takes one out by hand. An engineer lists the addresses of their own scopes
and removes none.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope staging
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may run list for a scope of their own; the other verbs need the superuser tier.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope staging
.SS Per-scope secret \fB(tacctl scope secret)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "show"
Print the scope's raw shared-secret value plus its length and posture.
The command runs as root, the store is mode 0600, and operators need
the raw value to paste into device configs — so
.B show
prints it rather than hiding it. Superuser only; an engineer may read the
secret of a scope of their own (another scope does not exist for them, and
each read is logged), and may not set or generate one.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope secret
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may run show for a scope of their own; set and generate need the superuser tier.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope secret
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "set " value
Set the scope's key after validation (≥
.B config secret-min-length
chars, mixed character classes). With RADIUS enabled, a secret FreeRADIUS
cannot carry (a backslash together with a
.BR $ )
fails the render and is refused;
.B "config render"
warns about one beyond the interoperability advice (63 printable
characters, no space). The secret is shared by TACACS+ and RADIUS.
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "generate"
Generate a strong key with
.B openssl rand -base64 24
and apply it.
.SS Per-scope protocols \fB(tacctl scope protocols)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "[list]"
Show which protocols serve the scope. Without a filter, every enabled
backend serves it.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope protocols
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope protocols
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "set " protocol\fR[,protocol...]
Limit the scope to the named protocols
.RB ( tacacs ", " radius ).
A backend whose protocol is not listed leaves the scope out of what it
renders: for TACACS+ that means its prefixes, its secret and its users'
grant of it are absent from tacquito.yaml; for RADIUS, its prefixes are not
clients. Refused when the list leaves out the scope's
.BR "scope auth-method" .
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "clear"
Remove the filter.
.SS Per-scope vendor attributes (RADIUS) \fB(tacctl scope vendor-attrs, tacctl scope devices)\fR
Over RADIUS an Access-Accept always carries
.B Service-Type
.RB ( Administrative-User
at priv-lvl 15, else
.BR NAS-Prompt-User ).
A vendor's privilege attribute is added only where the operator opted in:
.B Cisco-AVPair \(dqshell:priv-lvl=N\(dq
(cisco),
.B Juniper-Local-User-Name
(juniper),
.B WTI-Super
0\-3 (wti). An Access-Reject carries none. TACACS+ is not affected.
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope
Show the vendors enabled for the scope, or
.B "not sent"
(the state of a new scope).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope vendor-attrs
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope vendor-attrs
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope " enable|disable " vendor\fR[,vendor...]
Enable or disable a vendor's attribute for every device of the scope that
is not tagged. There is no
.BR set ,
.B clear
or
.BR none :
nothing is sent until it is enabled. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( vendor_attrs ),
so the change re-renders the RADIUS backend and restarts it.
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " [list]"
List the scope's tagged addresses.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope devices
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope devices
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " set " "ip\fR|\fIcidr vendor"
Tag an address (a bare IP is a /32 or /128) or a range of the scope
with its vendor: it gets that vendor's attribute and no other, whatever
the scope enables. The address must be one
.B "scope lookup"
answers with this scope: inside a prefix of it, and inside no more
specific prefix of another scope. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( devices ).
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " unset " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Remove a tag.
.B "scope prefixes remove"
refuses to leave a tag outside the scope's prefixes (unset it first, or
add the new prefix before removing the old one); tags go with the scope
on rename and remove.
.SS Per-scope idle-session timeout \fB(tacctl scope exec-timeout)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope exec-timeout " scope " [" minutes ]
Show or set the idle-session timeout (in minutes) that
.B "config cisco --scope " \fIscope\fR
and
.B "config juniper --scope " \fIscope\fR
render into device configs. Cisco emits
.BR "exec-timeout \fIn\fR 0"
on
.B line con 0
and
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos emits
.BR "set system login idle-timeout \fIn\fR" .
Range 0..60 (Junos's max). A value of
.B 0
disables idle expiry on both vendors. Default 60.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope exec-timeout
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope exec-timeout
.SS Per-scope mgmt-acl \fB(tacctl scope mgmt-acl)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope mgmt-acl " scope " " \
"<list|add|remove|clear|cisco-name|juniper-name>" " [" args "]"
Per-scope management ACL. Subcommand shape mirrors the global
.B "tacctl config mgmt-acl"
so operators who know one form know the other. The effective permit list of a
scope drives the Cisco VTY-ACL, the Junos filter and the ssh and https lines of
the WTI IP Tables list (Step 5 of
.BR "config wti" ).
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR list ,
.BR "add " <cidr>[,...] ,
.BR "remove " <cidr>[,...] ,
.B clear
— manage the per-scope permit list. Render fallback is per-scope
-> global
.RB ( mgmt_acl.permits )
-> empty. Empty per-scope lists prune the override so the scope
falls back to the global; `clear` is the explicit way to revert.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "cisco-name " [ label ] ,
.BR "juniper-name " [ label ]
— per-scope ACL / filter name. Fallback is per-scope -> global
.RB ( "config mgmt-acl cisco-name / juniper-name" )
-> shipped default (\fBVTY-ACL\fR for Cisco, \fBMGMT-ACL\fR for
Junos). Name must satisfy ACL-name rules (letter-start +
[A-Za-z0-9_-], 1..63 chars).
.RE
.TP
.IP
Cisco puts the effective name in
.B "ip access-list standard <NAME>"
and the matching
.B "access-class <NAME> in"
on
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos puts it in
.B "set firewall family inet filter <NAME>"
and the commented lo0 apply. Per-scope permits let one environment
deviate without changing the house-wide default.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope mgmt-acl
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope mgmt-acl
.SS Per-scope SNMP \fB(tacctl scope snmp)\fR
.PP
The SNMP settings of the devices of a scope. A setting the scope does not set
comes from the default
.RB ( "config snmp" ),
then the built-in; every
.B show
says which one a value is. The non-secret settings are
.B snmp_scope.\fIscope\fR.{version,port,timeout,v3.auth,v3.priv,contact,clients}
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
written only when set; the community and the v3 user with its passphrases are
the file
.IR /etc/tacctl/snmp/\fIscope\fR.yaml
(mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, the format of
.IR snmp.yaml ),
never printed but by
.BR "show \-\-reveal" .
A scope's rename moves them, its removal deletes them. The SNMP lookup of
.B "device add"
and
.B "device check"
uses the credentials of the device's scope (the one that answers its address;
a device in no scope uses the default). The verbs that set or clear anything,
and
.BR test ,
are the superuser's.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " show [ \-\-reveal ]"
The effective version, port, timeout, protocols, community or v3 user, contact
and allowed clients of the scope, each with where it comes from
.RB ( scope ,
.BR default ,
.BR built\-in ).
.B \-\-reveal
prints the credentials; an engineer may reveal the scopes of their own, and the
reveal is logged as
.BR "secret\-read kind=snmp" .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope snmp
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may read the settings of a scope of their own (show [\-\-reveal], version, port, timeout, contact, clients list); every setter, clear and test needs the superuser tier.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-reveal\fR
Print the community or the v3 passphrases (an engineer: their own scopes; logged).
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\fR
Remove the contact.
.TP
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
(community, v3\-user) Read the secrets from stdin, one per line.
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\fR \fIsha|sha256\fR
The authentication protocol: sha (HMAC\-SHA\-96) or sha256 (HMAC\-SHA\-256\-192) (default: the default's, else sha).
.TP
\fB\-\-priv\fR \fI<value>\fR
The privacy protocol: AES\-128 (the only one).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope snmp
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " version [ v2c | v3 ]"
Show or set the scope's SNMP version.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " community [ \-\-stdin ]"
Ask twice, without echo (or read one line from stdin), for the scope's v2c
community; the scope's version becomes
.BR v2c .
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " v3\-user " user " [ \-\-auth sha|sha256 ] [ \-\-priv aes128 ] [ \-\-stdin ]"
Set the scope's v3 user and its two passphrases (at least 8 characters); the
scope's version becomes
.BR v3 .
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " clients { list | add | remove } " [ cidr [ , cidr ...]]
The ranges allowed to query the scope's devices: IPv4 networks in canonical
form, kept in the order given, at most 32, never
.B 0.0.0.0/0
(the final refusal of everything else is always rendered and never stored)
and no IPv6. The tacctl server's own address is always the first client and is
not listed. An overlap of two ranges is a note, not an error. A scope without
ranges allows the tacctl server only, and the walkthroughs say so.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " contact [ " text " | \-\-clear ]"
Show, set or remove the scope's SNMP contact (up to 120 characters, no control
characters, no
.BR ? ).
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " port [ n ]"
Show or set the agents' UDP port for the scope.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " timeout [ seconds ]"
Show or set the wait for an answer for the scope, 1 to 10 seconds.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " clear"
Remove the scope's own settings, credentials, clients and contact: the default
applies again.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " test " address|device
Read the sysName of one address (or registered device) with the scope's
settings.
.SS Per-scope Cisco aaa-group-server label \fB(tacctl scope tacacs-group)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope tacacs-group " scope " [" name ]
Show or set the Cisco
.B aaa group server tacacs+ <NAME>
label for this scope. Rendered into every AAA group definition and
the method-list / accounting lines that reference the group. Per-scope
so each environment can match site naming conventions (e.g.
.BR TACACS_PROD ,
.BR ISE-GROUP ).
Must conform to Cisco ACL naming rules: letter-start, letters, digits,
and
.BR [_-] ;
1..63 chars. Default (unset)
.BR TACACS-GROUP .
Junos has no equivalent — its AAA configuration does not group named
servers this way.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope tacacs-group
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope tacacs-group
.SS Per-scope Cisco RADIUS group label \fB(tacctl scope radius-group)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope radius-group " scope " [" name ]
The RADIUS twin of
.BR "scope tacacs-group" :
show or set the Cisco
.B aaa group server radius <NAME>
label rendered by
.BR "config cisco --protocol radius" .
Same naming rules (letter-start, letters, digits and
.BR [_-] ;
1..63 chars). Default (unset)
.BR RADIUS-GROUP .
Junos has no equivalent. Stored as
.B radius_group.<scope>
in
.IR tacctl.yaml .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope radius-group
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope radius-group
.SS Per-scope default authentication method \fB(tacctl scope auth-method)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope auth-method " scope " [" tacacs | radius | clear ]
Show or set the protocol this scope uses when a command names none:
.BR "config cisco" ,
.B "config juniper"
and
.B "config wti"
without
.BR --protocol ,
.B "host enroll"
(for a host that is not registered yet) and
.B "config linux script"
without
.B --method
.RB ( tacacs
is host method
.BR tacplus ).
The order is: an explicit
.B --protocol
or
.BR --method ;
a registered host's own method; this setting; the one protocol the
scope's
.B protocols
filter names, when it names exactly one; the global default (device
configurations TACACS+, hosts
.BR "host default-method" ).
.B clear
removes the setting
.RB ( default
is accepted for it);
.B tacplus
is accepted for
.BR tacacs ,
which is the one spelling it prints.
Refused when the scope's
.B protocols
filter leaves the protocol out;
.B "scope protocols"
in turn refuses a list without the scope's auth-method.
.B "config cisco --legacy"
on a scope that resolves to RADIUS needs
.BR "--protocol tacacs" .
Stored as
.B scope_auth_method.<scope>
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ;
follows
.B "scope rename"
and is removed with the scope.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope auth-method
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope auth-method
.SS Per-scope AAA method-list order \fB(tacctl scope aaa-order)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope aaa-order " scope " [" tacacs-first | local-first ]
Show or set the order of methods in the Cisco
.B aaa authentication login
/
.B aaa authorization
lines and Junos
.B system authentication-order
emitted by
.B "config cisco --scope " \fIscope\fR
and
.B "config juniper --scope " \fIscope\fR .
Default (unset)
.B tacacs-first
keeps TACACS+ authoritative (local only kicks in on server outage).
Set
.B local-first
per-scope when a local break-glass account must authenticate even
while TACACS+ is reachable — any local username that collides with a
TACACS+ user will win locally on devices in this scope, so scope
local accounts to emergency / break-glass credentials only.
The change takes effect for the next
.B "config cisco"
/
.B "config juniper"
render of the named scope — existing device configs are unchanged
until the new method-list stanzas are pushed.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope aaa-order
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope aaa-order
.SS Per-scope break-glass local users \fB(tacctl scope breakglass)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope breakglass " scope " [" list ]
.TQ
.BI "scope breakglass " scope " add " name " [" "--role admin" | operator | readonly ]
.TQ
.BI "scope breakglass " scope " remove " name
List, add or remove the local accounts a scope's devices keep for the day the
server cannot be reached. Only the names and roles are recorded; the role
defaults to
.BR admin .
A name must be a device-safe user name (a letter, then up to 31 letters,
digits,
.B _
and
.BR - ),
must not be a user of the scope, and must not collide with a
template-user or class name of the Juniper walkthrough or be one of the
reserved names
.BR root " and " tacquito
or the Junos account
.B remote
(the template account of every remote user without a local-user-name). Names
compare without regard to case.
.BR "user add" ,
.BR "user scope" " (" add " and " replace )
and
.B "user rename"
refuse a user name equal to a break-glass account of a scope the user is in or
would be in, and name the
.B remove
verb as the way out. At most 16 users per scope. Stored as
.B breakglass_scope.<scope>.users
(a list of
.IR name : role )
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
written only when set; it follows
.B "scope rename"
and is removed with the scope. Superuser only.
.IP
.B tacctl never stores a password or hash.
.B "config cisco" ,
.B "config juniper"
and
.B "config wti"
render the account lines for the scope with a placeholder where the credential
goes (commented out, so a paste does not create an account with a placeholder
for a password) and list each recorded account on an
.B "Unfilled break-glass credentials"
line (tacctl cannot see whether a credential was put in, so the line stays for
as long as the account is recorded). Cisco:
.B "username <name> privilege <15|7|1> secret 9 <TYPE9-HASH>"
(the hash itself, as
.B "show running-config"
prints it:
.B "algorithm-type scrypt secret"
would take a plaintext password and hash it again; IOS 12.x with
.B --legacy
uses
.BR "secret 5" ),
next to the
.B "aaa authentication login default"
line that follows the scope's
.BR aaa-order .
Junos:
.B "set system login user <name> class <class> authentication encrypted-password '<HASH>'"
with the local class of the built-in
.BR superuser ,
.B operator
or
.B readonly
group for
.BR admin ,
.B operator
and
.BR readonly .
WTI: a local account of the matching access level (admin is Administrator, or
the level the group's
.B wti-level
sets, as in the mapping table of the same output) in a menu step, with
.B <PASSWORD>
where the password goes. A scope without break-glass users gets the existing advice and a
notice, and
.B "config validate"
warns, on one line naming the scopes (it is a warning, not an error), that they are a lockout risk;
.B "scope show"
says which scope has none. The credential on the device is never created,
rotated or removed by tacctl.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope breakglass
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-role\fR \fI<role>\fR
(add) Admin (default), operator or readonly.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope breakglass
.SH DEVICE REGISTRY
The
.I device registry
gives names to the network devices that authenticate against this server, so
that they can be listed, looked up and acknowledged by name. It is kept in
.I /etc/tacctl/devices.yaml
(mode 0600, root only) and never touches
.IR store.yaml :
a device's scope and vendor tag are looked up from the store each time they are
shown, not stored, and registering a device renders nothing and restarts
nothing. Every write takes a snapshot first (see
.BR "backup list" ).
.PP
A device is found by name or by its registered address; names are compared
without regard to case. Enrolled Linux hosts
.RB ( "tacctl host" )
share the namespace: they are listed and shown as
.B linux
devices, read-only, and a device cannot take a host's name (nor
.B "host enroll \-\-name"
a device's). An address is registered at most once. An address that is not
registered is not found, even when a scope covers it.
.PP
A name that is a factory or image default (\c
.BR switch ,
.BR router ,
.BR cisco ,
.BR ubuntu ,
.B ip\-10\-0\-0\-1
and the like; matched without regard to case) is refused with the command that
gives the device a name of its own, unless
.B \-\-allow\-generic
is given; the device then carries a
.B generic-name
notice. A host enrolled under such a name before the registry existed is not
refused, and carries the notice. The built-in list can be extended with
.B generic_names:
(regular expressions matched against the whole name) in
.IR devices.yaml .
.PP
Host keys are pinned when a device is registered: tacctl reads the keys the
device offers with
.BR ssh\-keyscan (1)
(as root, outbound, without logging in), keeps them in
.IR devices.yaml ,
and prints their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints to be compared with the device console. Enrolled hosts are pinned
by
.B "host enroll"
and
.BR "host sync" .
Every registry write regenerates
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts ,
which lists exactly the pinned keys under each device's name, for
.BI "ssh \-o UserKnownHostsFile=/var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts \-o GlobalKnownHostsFile=none \-o HostKeyAlias=" name " \-o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes" .
A pin changes only through
.BR "device hostkey accept" " or " "device hostkey set" ,
never by itself.
.PP
What the daemons saw of each device is kept in the
.I seen cache
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-seen.json ),
which
.B "device scan"
fills from the logs: the tacquito journal (the
.BI "accepting user [" user "] from [" address "]"
and
.B "failed to validate the user"
lines, the
.B "bad secret detected for ip"
and
.BI "remote [" address "] has no secret providers"
errors, and at log level 30 the
.B "prefix secret provider matches remote"
lines) and FreeRADIUS's
.I tacctl\-auth.log
with its rotations. For each address it keeps the first and last sighting,
the count, the last user, outcome and backend, and the last NAS-Identifier.
.B list
and
.B show
read the cache only, and say how old it is.
.PP
Notices are computed each time they are shown; only acknowledgements are
stored. Besides the registry's own
.RB ( generic-name ,
.BR hostkey-unpinned ,
and for an enrolled host
.BR address\-changed :
its address changed between two enrolments or syncs),
the seen cache raises
.B ambiguous-nas-id
(one NAS-Identifier sent from several addresses; a fully qualified host name
on each device tells them apart),
.B generic-nas-id
(a device identifying itself by a generic name),
.B name-mismatch
(a NAS-Identifier other than the registry name or the hostname, either in full
or by its first label, as
.B sw1
for
.BR sw1.site-a.example ;
informational),
.B duplicate-address
(two entries at one address, or an address identifying as another entry),
.B identity-changed
(the NAS-Identifier an address sends changed: a replaced or reset unit), and
the host-key re-scan of
.B "device scan"
and
.B "device check"
raises
.B hostkey-changed
(no pinned key offered, or another key of a pinned type; it cannot be
acknowledged, only
.B "device hostkey accept"
or
.B set
clears it),
.B hostkey-added
(a new key type, the pins unchanged) and
.B hostkey-unreachable
(no key could be read; with the last good scan). A scan never changes a pin.
Notices appear in the scan output,
.BR "tacctl status" ,
.BR "device list" ,
.B "device notices"
and
.B "device show"
(which counts the acknowledged ones;
.B \-\-all
on either lists them, marked).
.PP
Reading verbs
.RB ( list ,
.BR show ,
.BR notices ,
.BR ssh\-config )
and
.B "tacctl ssh"
are open to the read-only and operator tiers, which see only the devices and
hosts whose scope is one of their own; an entry no scope covers is seen by
administrators only.
.BR export ,
.BR scan ,
.BR discover ,
.B check
and
.B "list \-\-scan" | "\-\-probe"
are operator-level (the scan output's notices are filtered the same way;
.B discover
lists every address). Everything else needs an administrator.
.SS Device registry \fB(tacctl device)\fR
.TP
.B device
With no subcommand (or with
.BR help ),
prints a usage summary and exits 0.
.TP
.BI "device list " "[ \-\-stale ] [ \-\-unconfigured ] [ \-\-scan ] [ \-\-probe ] [ \-\-json ]"
One row per registered device, then per enrolled host: name, address, vendor,
scope, state, last seen, by, via, and the open notices. The state is
.B configured
when a scope's prefixes cover the address (a host: when its scope exists) and
.B unconfigured
otherwise.
.B \-\-unconfigured
lists only those;
.B \-\-stale
lists the devices whose last sighting is older than
.BR stale\-days ,
which are also marked
.BR stale .
The last seen column is the time of the last sighting,
.BI "rejected " time
when the last exchange was a refusal (with
.B "(bad secret)"
or
.B "(no scope)"
when the device's packets could not be read or no scope covers it), and
.B never
for an address no scan has seen; with no seen data at all the columns print
.BR \- .
The footer says when the seen data was read.
.B \-\-scan
runs
.B "device scan"
first.
.B \-\-probe
adds a
.B REACH
column: a TCP connect to each entry's ssh port, 3 seconds, all at once
.RB ( open ,
.BR closed ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR unreachable ).
This server often has no route to the devices' management ports, so a timeout
may be a false alarm.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device list
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-stale\fR
Only the devices not seen for stale\-days.
.TP
\fB\-\-unconfigured\fR
Only the devices no scope's prefixes cover.
.TP
\fB\-\-scan\fR
Scan the logs first (as 'device scan').
.TP
\fB\-\-probe\fR
Add REACH: a TCP connect to each ssh port (3 s).
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
(list, show, export, check) Print JSON.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command device list
.TP
.BI "device show " "name|address " [ \-\-all ] " " [ \-\-json ]
One device or host in full: address, hostname, vendor, port, legacy ssh,
description, the scope that answers the address and the prefix that routes it
(and the scopes it shadows), the vendor tag that covers it, the pinned host keys,
the last and first sighting with the sighting count and the NAS-Identifier the
device sends, and the open notices with a count of the acknowledged ones
.RB ( \-\-all
lists them too, marked). It warns when the
vendor tag and the registered vendor disagree.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device show
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
(list, show, export, check) Print JSON.
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
The acknowledged notices too.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command device show
.TP
.BI "device add " "[ name ] address " "[ \-\-vendor cisco|juniper|wti|other ] [ \-\-hostname " dns " ] [ \-\-port " n " ] [ \-\-description " text " ] [ \-\-snmp\-location " text " ] [ \-\-legacy\-ssh ] [ \-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint " | \-\-no\-host\-key ] [ \-\-no\-lookup ] [ \-\-allow\-generic ]"
Register a device. A name is letters, digits,
.RB ` . ` ,
.RB ` _ `
and
.RB ` - ` ,
starting with a letter or digit, at most 253 characters, each dotted part at
most 63: a fully qualified host name such as
.B sw1.site-a.example
is a name. The vendor defaults to
.B other
(and only drives the ssh profile; it does not tag the address in the scope).
The address is stored canonically (IPv6 in compressed lower-case form) and may
carry no prefix length. A name or address that is taken, a reserved word (a
tacctl command, \c
.BR local ,
.BR all ),
and a generic name are refused. The host keys the device offers at its address
and ssh port are read with
.B "ssh\-keyscan \-T 5 \-p"
.I port
.B "\-t ed25519,ecdsa,rsa"
(with
.BR \-\-legacy\-ssh ,
the
.B ssh\-rsa
type is asked for by name too) and pinned, and their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints are printed with the command that shows them on the device's
console; compare them before the first connection. With
.BI "\-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint
the device is registered only when it offers a key with that fingerprint, and
only that key is pinned (the other keys it offers are listed, unpinned). With
.B \-\-no\-host\-key
nothing is scanned: the device is registered unpinned and carries a
.B hostkey-unpinned
notice. A device that does not answer the scan, or offers no key matching
.BR \-\-host\-key ,
is refused and nothing is written. The output says which scope, if any, answers
the address.
.IP
Next to the scan,
.B add
reads the device's own name, its SNMP
.B sysName.0
(see
.BR "config snmp" ),
and prints it
.RI ( "The device calls itself '" sysname "' (SNMP sysName)." ).
When it is neither the name given, the
.BR \-\-hostname ,
nor the first label of either (compared without regard to case), a warning
names the fixes; the add goes ahead. A device that does not answer within the
timeout (one retry), an empty sysName, or no SNMP set up is one info line
.RI ( "No SNMP answer from " address "; no name hint." );
when a scan recorded a NAS-Identifier for the address, that is shown instead,
labelled as such. Nothing of it is stored.
.B \-\-no\-lookup
skips it. With no
.IR name ,
the sysName, lowercased, is offered as the name and taken only after a
.B y
at a terminal; without a terminal, without an answer, or when the sysName is
not a valid device name or is a generic one (a WTI unit's Site ID
.BR WTI ),
the add is refused with the usage line. The offered name goes through every
other check of the add.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device add
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-vendor\fR \fIcisco|juniper|wti|other\fR
The vendor (default other).
.TP
\fB\-\-hostname\fR \fI<dns>\fR
The device's DNS name.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<n>\fR
Its ssh port (default 22).
.TP
\fB\-\-description\fR \fI<text>\fR
A description of the device.
.TP
\fB\-\-snmp\-location\fR \fI<text>\fR
Its place (the SNMP location the walkthroughs render).
.TP
\fB\-\-legacy\-ssh\fR
Old IOS: SHA\-1 key exchange and ssh\-rsa.
.TP
\fB\-\-host\-key\fR \fISHA256:<fp>\fR
Register only if the device offers this key; pin it alone.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-host\-key\fR
Register without a pinned key (a hostkey\-unpinned notice).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-lookup\fR
Do not read the device's own name by SNMP (sysName).
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-generic\fR
(add, rename, import) Allow a generic name such as 'switch'.
.RE
